1
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:04,195
<i>Every time I'm out</i>
<i>It's not where I want to be</i>

2
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:12,876
<i>That's okay with me because</i>
<i>He's not the only one</i>

3
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:14,593
<i>Party girl</i>

4
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:16,759
<i>Lay off on the party, girl</i>

5
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:18,996
<i>Come and get your party, girl</i>

6
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:20,713
<i>I become the party girl</i>

7
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:23,358
<i>No one wants a party girl</i>

8
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:26,752
<i>Come and get a party, girl</i>

9
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,230
Ricky, you want to check
the level on that mike?

10
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:35,915
And you guys, pick up the tempo
a little bit next time, okay?

11
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:41,075
- Angel.
- I'm not here.

12
00:01:41,240 --> 00:01:44,596
Hey, hey, hey, where are you going?

13
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:46,991
- Out.
- I've got interviews set up for you

14
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:48,559
this afternoon and photos tonight.

15
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:53,191
Look, Lou, I told you I'd come down
and do your benefit, but that's it, okay?

16
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:56,113
Angel, it's all arranged.

17
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:58,919
You should have asked me first.
Sorry.

18
00:01:59,080 --> 00:02:01,389
Angel, I'm warning you.
Don't give me a hard time.

19
00:02:01,560 --> 00:02:04,154
- You'll do the interviews.
- Stop it, Lou.

20
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:06,709
You don't own me!

21
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:09,317
Where are you going?

22
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:13,195
I'm going to party until I drop.

23
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:16,038
Lou?

24
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:22,392
Lou.

25
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:51,677
I need the police.

26
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:54,274
Yeah, it's an emergency.

27
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:17,871
Miss Danello?

28
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:20,910
Margaret Danello?

29
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:23,036
The name's Angel.

30
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:24,428
May I talk to you?

31
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:26,753
Maybe later, okay?

32
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:29,871
This can't wait.

33
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:32,633
Homicide.

34
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:37,598
I'd like to ask you a few questions
about Lou Pearson.

35
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:47,233
- Ben? We just got a new client.
- Yeah?

36
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:48,992
Max Weinberg
represents her in New York,

37
00:04:49,160 --> 00:04:50,832
called and asked us
to take her case.

38
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,558
Who is it?

39
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:54,392
Top recording star,

40
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:57,597
dozens of magazine covers,
sold-out concert tours.

41
00:04:57,760 --> 00:04:59,273
Am I going to have to guess
her name?

42
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:01,317
She's waiting in my office right now.

43
00:05:01,480 --> 00:05:06,474
I just-- Remember how you always say
justice is blind?

44
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:10,633
Charlene, the suspense
is overwhelming. Who is this person?

45
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:20,557
Ben, this is Angel.

46
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:24,315
Hi, Ben.

47
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:31,148
What is this place,
some kind of a museum?

48
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:34,750
I love peanuts.

49
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:40,069
No.

50
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:41,593
- Ben.
- No.

51
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:43,591
She's a very big star.

52
00:05:43,760 --> 00:05:46,115
- I never heard of her.
- Oh, Dad.

53
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:50,155
- What is she, rock 'n' roll?
- Yes, and very popular.

54
00:05:50,320 --> 00:05:53,949
Draws crowds of 30, 40,
50,000 people.

55
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:56,479
You're kidding.

56
00:05:58,240 --> 00:06:00,276
Now, this is old.

57
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:03,714
There'll be a very big retainer.

58
00:06:07,720 --> 00:06:11,076
So you want to get started?

59
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:17,311
Having Lou for a manager was sort of
like signing a pact with the devil.

60
00:06:17,480 --> 00:06:19,630
Was there a romantic involvement?

61
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:21,229
No.

62
00:06:21,400 --> 00:06:23,675
What time did you arrive
at his house last night?

63
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:25,637
I'm not sure.

64
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:28,597
It was after 1 2, I think.

65
00:06:28,760 --> 00:06:30,910
No, wait, it was after 1 .

66
00:06:31,080 --> 00:06:32,559
How is it you don't remember?

67
00:06:33,880 --> 00:06:35,074
I was partying.

68
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:39,309
I went to a lot of different clubs
and I drank a little too much,

69
00:06:39,480 --> 00:06:43,155
and I went to Lou's house
to patch things up, and then I crashed.

70
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:45,950
Lou was still up
when I sort of passed out.

71
00:06:47,440 --> 00:06:48,634
Look, could I go now?

72
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:51,992
Perhaps you don't recognize
the gravity of your situation.

73
00:06:52,560 --> 00:06:54,152
The police let me go.

74
00:06:54,320 --> 00:06:58,393
For the moment.
The police might want you back.

75
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:01,754
You had a bitter argument
with Mr. Pearson yesterday.

76
00:07:02,120 --> 00:07:04,270
You went to his house.
You're not sure when.

77
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:07,518
You found the body. You ran.

78
00:07:07,920 --> 00:07:09,478
If I were a policeman,

79
00:07:09,640 --> 00:07:14,077
that would make you a prime suspect
in this case, Miss Danello.

80
00:07:14,480 --> 00:07:17,870
Please, call me Angel.

81
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,230
The name on the docket
will be Margaret Danello.

82
00:07:21,400 --> 00:07:22,594
That's fine with me.

83
00:07:23,320 --> 00:07:27,393
If I were you, I'd try real hard
to remember where I was last night.

84
00:07:28,120 --> 00:07:32,796
Look, maybe I don't remember exactly
where I was or when I was there,

85
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:36,191
but I think I'd remember
if I murdered Lou, okay?

86
00:07:36,920 --> 00:07:38,512
You know...

87
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:44,790
...every case we take on
requires a little different approach.

88
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:48,393
Perhaps you can jog
Miss Danello's memory.

89
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:51,996
I'll see what the police have to say.
Excuse me.

90
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:03,439
You know, he's kind of a pain.

91
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:06,236
He grows on you.

92
00:08:25,160 --> 00:08:26,798
What are you doing here, Ben?

93
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:28,791
We don't have enough
to arrest your client yet.

94
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:31,269
That never stopped you before.

95
00:08:32,400 --> 00:08:35,551
- Who is she?
- That's the widow over there.

96
00:08:35,720 --> 00:08:38,553
They've been separated
for about five months.

97
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:44,319
- Any prints on these glasses?
- Nothing good.

98
00:08:46,080 --> 00:08:49,516
Looks like Lou Pearson
knew whoever it was.

99
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:53,715
- Seemed like they were celebrating.
- Sure looks that way.

100
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:59,149
- He was stabbed, wasn't he?
- In the chest.

101
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:03,075
There's a pair of scissors
missing from this desk set.

102
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:05,196
We're looking for them.

103
00:09:09,760 --> 00:09:11,478
- Mrs. Pearson?
- Yes?

104
00:09:11,640 --> 00:09:14,712
I'm Ben Matlock, Margaret--
Angel's lawyer.

105
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:17,348
Well, I just told them
everything I had to say.

106
00:09:17,520 --> 00:09:19,511
Well, only the good die young.

107
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:22,719
You one of Pearson's clients?

108
00:09:22,880 --> 00:09:25,394
I was more than a client.
We're like brothers.

109
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:29,517
Yeah, like Cain and Abel.

110
00:09:31,680 --> 00:09:33,750
Little upset, huh?

111
00:09:39,120 --> 00:09:41,031
- You Detective Davis?
- Yeah.

112
00:09:41,200 --> 00:09:42,394
Yeah, I just heard.

113
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:43,993
Excuse me, who are you?

114
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:48,236
Sonny Hutton.
I just flew in for the concert.

115
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:51,676
I was on my way from the airport when
I got the news. I can't believe this.

116
00:09:51,840 --> 00:09:54,115
His secretary says
there's a tape of yours missing.

117
00:09:54,760 --> 00:09:57,877
My tape? They stole my tape?

118
00:09:59,080 --> 00:10:00,115
The new album.

119
00:10:00,280 --> 00:10:02,555
Hey, if it sells like your last album,
they'll lose money.

120
00:10:03,560 --> 00:10:05,630
Look, we still got work to do here.

121
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:09,190
Hey, at least you know one thing
about the killer.

122
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:12,989
If the guy took Sonny's tape,
he's got no taste.

123
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,434
And you can quote me as saying
that I'll be a party girl

124
00:10:22,600 --> 00:10:25,398
until my party is over.

125
00:10:28,120 --> 00:10:31,669
- Hey! That was a wire service!
- I don't care if it was Tom Brokaw,

126
00:10:31,840 --> 00:10:34,752
- and get your feet off my desk.
- Boy, have you got a lot of nerve!

127
00:10:34,920 --> 00:10:37,639
If I'm going to represent you,
you're going to have to straighten up

128
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:39,995
- and do as you're told.
- And what if I don't want to?

129
00:10:40,160 --> 00:10:41,639
You'd better do as he says, miss.

130
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:44,872
- What's that supposed to mean?
- Found the missing pair of scissors.

131
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:46,678
They were the murder weapon,
all right.

132
00:10:46,840 --> 00:10:49,149
The lab says they have
Pearson's blood type on them.

133
00:10:49,320 --> 00:10:50,878
Where'd they turn up, Rupert?

134
00:10:51,040 --> 00:10:53,508
Miss Danello's hotel room.

135
00:10:55,120 --> 00:10:57,236
You're under arrest for murder.

136
00:11:05,640 --> 00:11:07,835
- How much was your bail, Angel?
- Two hundred thousand.

137
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:09,911
- I'm an expensive date.
- So why'd you do it?

138
00:11:10,080 --> 00:11:11,718
Guys are always dropping dead
at my feet.

139
00:11:11,880 --> 00:11:13,791
Hold it, hold it, hold it.

140
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:18,316
Miss Danello is innocent
of Mr. Pearson's murder.

141
00:11:18,480 --> 00:11:21,119
Nonetheless, she takes the matter
very seriously.

142
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:24,158
At the moment, she is still shocked
by this tragic event

143
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:25,799
and has no comment.

144
00:11:25,960 --> 00:11:28,394
Isn't he adorable?

145
00:11:31,880 --> 00:11:33,757
Don't you ever do that.

146
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:35,797
I was just giving them
what they wanted.

147
00:11:35,960 --> 00:11:37,916
It's part of the game. It's the show.

148
00:11:38,360 --> 00:11:41,238
You keep forgetting you're not
on a stage. You're in a courtroom.

149
00:11:41,400 --> 00:11:44,198
- You're on trial for murder.
- Relax.

150
00:11:44,360 --> 00:11:46,510
- The press loves me.
- Ben.

151
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:49,591
Read her the headline.

152
00:11:49,760 --> 00:11:52,320
''The Party's Over for Party Girl.''

153
00:11:52,480 --> 00:11:55,756
They love you so much they're going
to convict you before we pick a jury.

154
00:11:55,920 --> 00:11:58,150
Lighten up, okay?

155
00:11:58,320 --> 00:12:00,197
We're going to set up
some rules right now.

156
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:03,318
Rule number one: you are not
to talk about this with anyone,

157
00:12:03,480 --> 00:12:04,879
especially not the press.

158
00:12:05,040 --> 00:12:07,235
Rule number two: the next time
we go in the courtroom,

159
00:12:07,400 --> 00:12:09,391
you're going to wear
more than just your underwear.

160
00:12:09,560 --> 00:12:12,120
Rule number three: the next time
you tell me to lighten up,

161
00:12:12,280 --> 00:12:14,111
you're going to find yourself
without a lawyer,

162
00:12:14,280 --> 00:12:16,475
without a defence,
and without a prayer. Got it?

163
00:12:18,440 --> 00:12:21,273
There she is,
an angel if ever I saw one.

164
00:12:21,440 --> 00:12:23,635
It is a privilege
and an honour to meet you.

165
00:12:23,800 --> 00:12:25,199
I am perhaps your greatest fan.

166
00:12:25,360 --> 00:12:27,669
- Tyler Hudson at your service.
- Who is this?

167
00:12:27,840 --> 00:12:29,910
I am their personal
private investigator,

168
00:12:30,080 --> 00:12:32,833
but most of my time
is spent investing venture capital.

169
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:36,197
But sometimes I take on
a special case that interests me.

170
00:12:36,360 --> 00:12:40,399
And Angel, this is for you,
genuine Georgia pecans.

171
00:12:40,560 --> 00:12:42,232
Something I might market as a gift.

172
00:12:42,400 --> 00:12:43,913
Can we get down to business?

173
00:12:44,440 --> 00:12:45,793
Now, the first thing we have to do

174
00:12:45,960 --> 00:12:48,952
is establish where you were
the night of the murder.

175
00:12:49,120 --> 00:12:52,192
What does the medical examiner
have to say?

176
00:12:52,720 --> 00:12:53,914
Tyler!

177
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:57,959
Oh, yeah. That Pearson died between
2 and 3:30 in the morning.

178
00:12:58,120 --> 00:13:00,350
I want you to think very hard.

179
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:03,080
Where were you between
2 and 3:30 yesterday morning?

180
00:13:03,240 --> 00:13:06,391
I already told you where I was.
I was partying and I don't remember.

181
00:13:06,560 --> 00:13:09,711
Look, if we can find someone who was
with you, you might be off the hook.

182
00:13:09,880 --> 00:13:12,474
Okay. Okay, okay.

183
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:17,714
I was at the Hard Line at 1 1 .

184
00:13:18,800 --> 00:13:20,552
No, that was Tuesday.

185
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:25,473
Okay, I know that I was
at the Dirt Club.

186
00:13:26,160 --> 00:13:27,354
That was on Monday.

187
00:13:29,640 --> 00:13:31,676
I'm not sure where I was.

188
00:13:32,320 --> 00:13:35,153
We're going to have to retrace
your steps.

189
00:13:35,320 --> 00:13:37,709
We have to know where everybody
was the night of the murder.

190
00:13:37,880 --> 00:13:41,668
The widow, Janice,
Sonny Hutton, Fancy Dan.

191
00:13:41,840 --> 00:13:43,398
- Danny Blaster.
- Whatever.

192
00:13:43,560 --> 00:13:45,198
He was in town that night.

193
00:13:45,360 --> 00:13:47,316
You're going to have to go with us.

194
00:13:47,480 --> 00:13:48,993
Can l--?

195
00:13:50,120 --> 00:13:52,236
All right. I'm on my way out of here.

196
00:13:52,400 --> 00:13:55,790
Angel, do you ever do
endorsements?

197
00:13:55,960 --> 00:13:58,679
Dad, I can't make it tonight.
I have an appointment.

198
00:13:58,840 --> 00:14:00,751
You're going to leave me
alone with her?

199
00:14:00,920 --> 00:14:04,629
She's not exactly dangerous.
I think you can protect yourself.

200
00:14:04,800 --> 00:14:06,950
It's not what she might do to me
that concerns me.

201
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:09,315
It's the other way around.
What a pain.

202
00:14:09,480 --> 00:14:12,756
She grows on you. Have fun.

203
00:14:14,760 --> 00:14:17,274
You've got to be kidding.

204
00:14:17,440 --> 00:14:19,749
How many more
of these places are there?

205
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:23,276
Not enough.
This is just a private party.

206
00:14:24,600 --> 00:14:25,953
You're kidding.

207
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:35,316
Hey, baby.

208
00:14:35,480 --> 00:14:37,755
Dan, you're looking good.

209
00:14:38,280 --> 00:14:41,716
Dan, this is Ben, my lawyer.

210
00:14:41,880 --> 00:14:44,394
- Yeah, we've met.
- Yeah.

211
00:14:44,560 --> 00:14:46,949
Listen, you need anything,
just let me know.

212
00:14:47,120 --> 00:14:49,315
Can you account for your time
the night of the murder?

213
00:14:49,480 --> 00:14:50,879
Why are you asking me?

214
00:14:51,880 --> 00:14:53,677
I don't know.
You said you'd like to help.

215
00:14:53,840 --> 00:14:55,319
It might help. You never know.

216
00:14:58,360 --> 00:14:59,873
Let's go.

217
00:15:04,640 --> 00:15:06,835
Got a little bit of a temper,
doesn't he?

218
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:08,911
- Yeah, I guess so.
- Yeah.

219
00:15:09,080 --> 00:15:11,116
I'll be over there.

220
00:15:19,560 --> 00:15:22,518
- Hi.
- Hi.

221
00:15:22,680 --> 00:15:25,877
- I thought you had an appointment.
- Oh, I do.

222
00:15:29,280 --> 00:15:32,590
- A musician?
- No, a tax lawyer. How you doing?

223
00:15:35,040 --> 00:15:37,793
Okay. I'll see you in the morning.

224
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:40,392
Early in the morning.

225
00:15:40,920 --> 00:15:43,309
Early. Right.

226
00:15:52,280 --> 00:15:53,793
Hi.

227
00:15:54,720 --> 00:15:58,110
- I saw you yesterday at Lou Pearson's.
- Oh, yes.

228
00:15:58,280 --> 00:16:00,111
I'm Ben Matlock, Angel's lawyer.

229
00:16:00,280 --> 00:16:02,430
- How you doing?
- Okay.

230
00:16:02,760 --> 00:16:05,832
Any luck finding
that missing tape of yours?

231
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,594
They looked everywhere
in the house. It's gone.

232
00:16:08,760 --> 00:16:13,311
Oh, I'm surprised record bootleggers
would be that interested.

233
00:16:13,480 --> 00:16:17,393
- Why is that?
- Your last album didn't do all that well.

234
00:16:18,600 --> 00:16:19,874
Is that supposed to be a dig?

235
00:16:20,520 --> 00:16:22,988
Oh, no. No, no, no.
I was just curious.

236
00:16:23,160 --> 00:16:25,674
My daughter tells me
that Dan Blaster's really hot right now,

237
00:16:25,840 --> 00:16:27,990
and I wondered
why they didn't take his tape.

238
00:16:28,160 --> 00:16:31,630
Maybe they listened to it first.

239
00:16:31,800 --> 00:16:34,872
When did you arrive in Atlanta,
Mr. Hutton?

240
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:39,792
I arrived Wednesday
morning, 1 0 a.m.

241
00:16:40,280 --> 00:16:45,274
Georgia Air flight 1 83 from New York.
You can check the airline on that.

242
00:16:46,200 --> 00:16:49,272
I think that puts me in the morning
after Lou was murdered,

243
00:16:49,440 --> 00:16:51,271
to answer your question.

244
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:53,879
I guess you're right.

245
00:16:54,040 --> 00:16:55,519
Clear out, clear out. Let's go.

246
00:16:58,120 --> 00:16:59,712
All right. Clear out, clear out.
Let's go.

247
00:16:59,880 --> 00:17:02,519
Hey, hey, hey, cut it out. Cut it out.

248
00:17:06,920 --> 00:17:10,469
I noticed you two
didn't really get along.

249
00:17:59,240 --> 00:18:02,391
You want to know where
I was the night of the murder, huh?

250
00:18:02,680 --> 00:18:03,874
It might help.

251
00:18:06,080 --> 00:18:08,310
Why don't you ask my friends?

252
00:18:08,480 --> 00:18:09,879
Ask me if you
wanna know something.

253
00:18:10,080 --> 00:18:11,274
No problem.

254
00:18:13,040 --> 00:18:15,508
Hey, aren't you a little old
to be out in this night air?

255
00:18:16,560 --> 00:18:17,993
You're probably right.

256
00:18:20,840 --> 00:18:22,034
I'll see you.

257
00:18:22,520 --> 00:18:23,839
Yeah?

258
00:18:25,680 --> 00:18:26,874
In court.

259
00:18:38,960 --> 00:18:41,599
Ben, I've got big news.

260
00:18:41,760 --> 00:18:43,478
Ron, this is Ben, my lawyer.

261
00:18:43,640 --> 00:18:47,076
I gave Angel a ride out
to Lou Pearson's house that night.

262
00:18:47,240 --> 00:18:50,789
- What time was that?
- Let's see...

263
00:18:50,960 --> 00:18:55,431
It was about 1 :30. Yeah, I'm a DJ
and I was on the air at 2.

264
00:18:55,600 --> 00:19:00,071
- I'm off tonight.
- Some other time, okay?

265
00:19:00,240 --> 00:19:02,151
Some other time.

266
00:19:03,240 --> 00:19:07,233
This places you at Lou Pearson's
at the time of the murder.

267
00:19:07,400 --> 00:19:09,516
It's big news, all right.
You don't have an alibi.

268
00:19:09,880 --> 00:19:13,634
- What?
- You don't have an alibi.

269
00:19:15,240 --> 00:19:16,992
Oh, yeah.

270
00:19:17,560 --> 00:19:20,028
Well, I'll see you tomorrow then.

271
00:19:21,560 --> 00:19:22,913
You'll see me tomorrow?

272
00:19:23,080 --> 00:19:24,911
Yeah. I'm going to go dance,

273
00:19:25,080 --> 00:19:27,389
and then they want me to do
a few songs.

274
00:19:29,880 --> 00:19:31,791
I'm a party girl, remember?

275
00:19:32,160 --> 00:19:35,072
Angel, we're leaving.

276
00:19:35,240 --> 00:19:36,309
What?

277
00:19:37,600 --> 00:19:39,033
Let's go.

278
00:19:42,440 --> 00:19:44,112
Thanks, Tom.

279
00:19:45,160 --> 00:19:46,718
Step right in here.

280
00:19:46,880 --> 00:19:49,440
What is this,
some kind of rehearsal?

281
00:19:50,760 --> 00:19:53,115
You know, I've had a few clients

282
00:19:53,280 --> 00:19:57,068
who didn't seem to realize
the gravity of their situation.

283
00:19:57,240 --> 00:19:59,879
I used to bring them in here
and sit them on a chair or a table

284
00:20:00,040 --> 00:20:03,874
and I'd browbeat them into realizing
just how tough this business can be.

285
00:20:04,040 --> 00:20:05,632
I don't do that anymore.

286
00:20:05,800 --> 00:20:07,631
One, because it wears me out.

287
00:20:07,800 --> 00:20:10,189
Two, because most people
have come to realize

288
00:20:10,360 --> 00:20:12,635
that prison is a mean,
foul-smelling place

289
00:20:12,800 --> 00:20:15,155
more horrible
than your worst nightmares.

290
00:20:15,320 --> 00:20:16,799
I'm not sure you do.

291
00:20:22,400 --> 00:20:25,790
- Unlock the door.
- I don't have the key.

292
00:20:27,480 --> 00:20:28,959
But that man does.

293
00:20:30,440 --> 00:20:32,158
That's true.

294
00:20:34,040 --> 00:20:38,079
Matlock, don't take all day.

295
00:21:00,920 --> 00:21:02,831
Matlock!

296
00:21:35,200 --> 00:21:37,953
- What kept you?
- Tom had stepped away.

297
00:21:38,120 --> 00:21:40,111
I hope you weren't frightened.

298
00:21:41,440 --> 00:21:43,635
- I got the message.
- Good, good.

299
00:21:43,800 --> 00:21:46,439
Because unless you start to look
and act like a lady,

300
00:21:46,600 --> 00:21:49,876
prison's where you're liable to be
for the next 50 years.

301
00:21:50,920 --> 00:21:52,956
Believe me...

302
00:21:55,320 --> 00:21:57,356
...it's no party.

303
00:22:18,040 --> 00:22:19,109
Yeah.

304
00:22:19,280 --> 00:22:20,713
- All right.
- Baby.

305
00:22:20,880 --> 00:22:21,869
Yeah.

306
00:22:27,880 --> 00:22:29,916
I thought I told you
to dress like a lady.

307
00:22:30,760 --> 00:22:32,034
I did.

308
00:22:33,640 --> 00:22:35,710
Your secret's safe with me.

309
00:22:37,760 --> 00:22:39,591
How was your evening?

310
00:22:39,760 --> 00:22:41,876
- Better than yours.
- I would hope.

311
00:22:43,400 --> 00:22:46,517
I found out that Danny Blaster
was having Lou Pearson audited.

312
00:22:47,080 --> 00:22:49,913
And that's why he has
all the bikers around him...

313
00:22:50,080 --> 00:22:51,718
...for protection.

314
00:22:51,880 --> 00:22:53,279
From Lou Pearson?

315
00:22:53,440 --> 00:22:56,796
Pearson was connected,
if you know what I mean.

316
00:22:56,960 --> 00:22:59,713
How did you get all this information?

317
00:23:00,160 --> 00:23:03,675
The tax lawyer I was with
is in the music business.

318
00:23:04,720 --> 00:23:06,836
Told you I had an appointment.

319
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:10,231
I found out something else.
Dan is a champagne drinker.

320
00:23:11,640 --> 00:23:13,551
So is Sonny Hutton.

321
00:23:15,400 --> 00:23:18,312
Mrs. Hoffman, how long had
you been Mr. Pearson's housekeeper?

322
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:20,869
- Over two years.
- And was anyone else in the house

323
00:23:21,040 --> 00:23:23,600
- at the time you discovered the body?
- No.

324
00:23:23,760 --> 00:23:25,239
Did you see anyone leave the house

325
00:23:25,400 --> 00:23:27,118
when you reported for work
that morning?

326
00:23:27,280 --> 00:23:30,352
I saw Angel, I mean Miss Danello.

327
00:23:31,600 --> 00:23:34,956
- The defendant?
- That's right. She was running away.

328
00:23:37,440 --> 00:23:39,396
No further questions, Your Honour.

329
00:23:39,560 --> 00:23:41,073
Your witness, Mr. Matlock.

330
00:23:41,240 --> 00:23:42,798
She never liked me.
Ask her about the--

331
00:23:42,960 --> 00:23:44,518
I'll take it from here, Margaret.

332
00:23:48,160 --> 00:23:49,991
Mrs. Hoffman...

333
00:23:51,240 --> 00:23:54,630
...was Mr. Pearson particular
about the way you kept his house?

334
00:23:54,800 --> 00:23:56,233
Yes, he was.

335
00:23:56,400 --> 00:23:59,233
Mrs. Hoffman, it's been stipulated
that these scissors,

336
00:23:59,400 --> 00:24:02,710
People's Exhibit 6, were the weapon
that killed Mr. Pearson.

337
00:24:02,880 --> 00:24:04,996
- Do you recognize them?
- Yes, I do.

338
00:24:05,160 --> 00:24:07,196
They were kept
on Mr. Pearson's desk,

339
00:24:07,360 --> 00:24:08,952
- were they not?
- Yes.

340
00:24:09,120 --> 00:24:10,872
You cleaned the desk
the day of the murder?

341
00:24:11,040 --> 00:24:13,873
- Yes, I did.
- What is that?

342
00:24:14,280 --> 00:24:15,998
It appears to be rust.

343
00:24:16,480 --> 00:24:19,756
But Mrs. Hoffman,
you testified that Mr. Pearson

344
00:24:19,920 --> 00:24:23,151
was a meticulous man.
You let his scissors get rusty?

345
00:24:23,320 --> 00:24:25,151
I did nothing of the kind.

346
00:24:26,440 --> 00:24:29,955
Well, metal only rusts
when it gets wet.

347
00:24:30,120 --> 00:24:32,111
You suppose someone
left them outside?

348
00:24:32,280 --> 00:24:35,272
Objection. Calls for speculation
on the part of the witness.

349
00:24:35,760 --> 00:24:36,954
Sustained.

350
00:24:37,120 --> 00:24:40,112
Mrs. Hoffman,
Sergeant Davis has testified

351
00:24:40,280 --> 00:24:44,398
that these scissors were found
in Miss Danello's hotel closet.

352
00:24:44,560 --> 00:24:46,551
How do you suppose
they got rusty in her closet?

353
00:24:46,720 --> 00:24:47,869
Objection.

354
00:24:48,040 --> 00:24:49,837
Speculation. Relevancy?

355
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,355
Your Honour, I am simply
trying to demonstrate

356
00:24:52,520 --> 00:24:54,431
that it is possible that these scissors

357
00:24:54,600 --> 00:24:58,593
were thrown away or hidden outside
someplace where they got wet.

358
00:24:58,760 --> 00:25:02,036
Then whoever killed Mr. Pearson
came back later, got them,

359
00:25:02,200 --> 00:25:04,919
planted them in the defendant's room.
That would explain the rust.

360
00:25:05,080 --> 00:25:08,117
Mr. Matlock, that does call
for speculation from this witness.

361
00:25:08,640 --> 00:25:09,993
Objection sustained.

362
00:25:11,720 --> 00:25:13,517
No further questions.

363
00:25:14,640 --> 00:25:16,153
I call Janice Pearson.

364
00:25:16,920 --> 00:25:18,990
Mrs. Pearson, how long
have you known the defendant?

365
00:25:19,160 --> 00:25:20,275
About three years.

366
00:25:20,440 --> 00:25:22,271
And in that time,
did your husband ever discuss

367
00:25:22,440 --> 00:25:24,829
his personal relationship
with Miss Danello?

368
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:27,230
- Yes, he did.
- What did he tell you?

369
00:25:27,400 --> 00:25:29,311
He said she had a violent temper,

370
00:25:29,480 --> 00:25:32,199
she was getting some bad advice
from other people,

371
00:25:32,360 --> 00:25:34,430
and he was afraid
of what she might do.

372
00:25:34,600 --> 00:25:36,192
- What? She's a liar.
- Sit down.

373
00:25:37,080 --> 00:25:40,789
Mr. Matlock, the bench cautions you
to control your client.

374
00:25:43,600 --> 00:25:44,999
Mrs. Pearson,
were you ever present

375
00:25:45,160 --> 00:25:47,720
when Miss Danello threatened
your late husband with bodily harm?

376
00:25:47,880 --> 00:25:49,154
Yes, I was.

377
00:25:49,880 --> 00:25:51,836
That is a lie. I never did.

378
00:25:52,440 --> 00:25:54,271
Mr. Matlock, I'm warning you--

379
00:25:54,440 --> 00:25:56,556
She's just trying to bury me.

380
00:25:56,720 --> 00:26:00,429
Another word from you, and I'll have
you removed from the courtroom.

381
00:26:00,600 --> 00:26:03,478
Your Honour, my client
is under considerable strain.

382
00:26:03,640 --> 00:26:05,198
I apologize for the interruption.

383
00:26:05,360 --> 00:26:06,793
It won't happen again.

384
00:26:06,960 --> 00:26:08,871
I will not tolerate another outburst.

385
00:26:09,680 --> 00:26:11,159
Now, please continue.

386
00:26:11,320 --> 00:26:13,675
No further questions, Your Honour.

387
00:26:14,160 --> 00:26:15,991
Mrs. Pearson...

388
00:26:16,160 --> 00:26:19,118
...have you ever been a patient
at Atlanta General Hospital?

389
00:26:19,280 --> 00:26:21,919
I think so. It was a long time ago.

390
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:25,389
I'm surprised
you don't remember better.

391
00:26:25,560 --> 00:26:29,155
According to these medical records,
you were a regular customer.

392
00:26:29,320 --> 00:26:32,392
In November, you checked in
with a fractured arm.

393
00:26:32,560 --> 00:26:34,949
In January, cracked ribs.

394
00:26:35,120 --> 00:26:37,759
In April, abrasions and bruises.

395
00:26:37,920 --> 00:26:39,717
How did all these things happen?

396
00:26:41,160 --> 00:26:42,991
Accidents.

397
00:26:47,400 --> 00:26:49,709
Mrs. Pearson...

398
00:26:49,880 --> 00:26:52,155
...isn't it true...

399
00:26:52,320 --> 00:26:55,995
...that you filed assault charges
against your husband

400
00:26:56,160 --> 00:26:57,798
in April of last year?

401
00:26:57,960 --> 00:26:59,518
Those charges were dropped.

402
00:26:59,680 --> 00:27:01,079
Answer the question.

403
00:27:01,240 --> 00:27:05,472
Isn't it true you filed assault charges
against your husband?

404
00:27:06,160 --> 00:27:07,673
- Yes.
- And isn't it true

405
00:27:07,840 --> 00:27:09,876
you filed those assault charges

406
00:27:10,040 --> 00:27:12,031
while you were being treated
for those injuries

407
00:27:12,200 --> 00:27:13,428
at Atlanta General Hospital?

408
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:16,672
- Objection. Relevancy.
- Your Honour,

409
00:27:16,840 --> 00:27:18,398
defence is attempting to show

410
00:27:18,560 --> 00:27:21,870
that Mr. Pearson was himself
prone to violence.

411
00:27:22,040 --> 00:27:25,237
Overruled. Answer the question.

412
00:27:26,240 --> 00:27:27,832
Could you repeat the question?

413
00:27:29,320 --> 00:27:32,551
''Isn't it true you filed those assault
charges while you were being treated

414
00:27:32,720 --> 00:27:35,029
for those injuries
at Atlanta General Hospital?''

415
00:27:37,280 --> 00:27:38,713
I suppose so.

416
00:27:40,440 --> 00:27:44,718
Then you withdrew those charges
two days later after you were released.

417
00:27:45,520 --> 00:27:46,999
That's correct.

418
00:27:47,400 --> 00:27:50,756
Mrs. Pearson, are you aware

419
00:27:50,920 --> 00:27:53,593
that you are the sole beneficiary

420
00:27:53,760 --> 00:27:56,194
- of your husband's estate?
- Objection, Your Honour.

421
00:27:56,360 --> 00:27:58,828
- Sustained.
- No more questions.

422
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:02,436
Court will adjourn until 9 a.m.
tomorrow morning.

423
00:28:05,160 --> 00:28:07,196
Is that it for today?

424
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:10,230
You did yourself
a lot of damage today.

425
00:28:11,240 --> 00:28:14,152
That outburst could cost us
the case with this jury.

426
00:28:14,320 --> 00:28:16,914
- She was lying.
- I don't care if she quoted the Bible.

427
00:28:17,400 --> 00:28:19,709
That jury's looking
at a spoiled, smart aleck kid

428
00:28:19,880 --> 00:28:21,154
who can't control her emotions

429
00:28:21,320 --> 00:28:23,117
maybe to the point
she could commit murder.

430
00:28:23,640 --> 00:28:25,039
Now, when you come
in here tomorrow,

431
00:28:25,200 --> 00:28:28,636
you better act right or we might as well
just throw in the towel.

432
00:28:30,920 --> 00:28:32,638
- Anything else?
- Yeah.

433
00:28:33,160 --> 00:28:36,311
Buy yourself a dress that doesn't
look like you belong in a circus.

434
00:28:39,600 --> 00:28:42,398
- Ben.
- Did I leave out something?

435
00:28:43,160 --> 00:28:45,754
No, you covered everything.

436
00:28:54,680 --> 00:28:58,116
- You all right?
- I'm fine, okay?

437
00:28:58,800 --> 00:29:01,234
- Angel, I know it's been rough on you.
- Look...

438
00:29:02,640 --> 00:29:06,189
...I'm sick and tired of you and
your father trying to scare me, okay?

439
00:29:07,880 --> 00:29:10,474
- I know how you must feel.
- No, you don't!

440
00:29:10,640 --> 00:29:12,870
You don't know anything about me!

441
00:29:13,200 --> 00:29:15,919
I know your father
was an alcoholic...

442
00:29:16,080 --> 00:29:18,469
...and your mother left
when you were 1 1 .

443
00:29:18,640 --> 00:29:21,632
I know you spent some time
in reform school.

444
00:29:22,880 --> 00:29:24,552
Yeah...

445
00:29:25,040 --> 00:29:27,031
...but you weren't there
when my old man came home

446
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:29,191
and beat up my mom every night.

447
00:29:29,360 --> 00:29:31,237
And you weren't there
when I got six months

448
00:29:31,400 --> 00:29:34,278
for riding around in a car
that I didn't know was stolen.

449
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:37,036
Nobody was there but me.

450
00:29:37,880 --> 00:29:40,440
You can't do it alone this time.

451
00:29:40,600 --> 00:29:43,831
If we're going to win this case,
you've got to work with us.

452
00:29:46,560 --> 00:29:48,198
You know something?

453
00:29:50,120 --> 00:29:54,432
You're a lot slicker,
but you're just like him.

454
00:29:56,960 --> 00:29:58,109
Thank you.

455
00:30:01,080 --> 00:30:02,479
You're welcome.

456
00:30:16,080 --> 00:30:18,355
How many of those pecans
did you say you bought?

457
00:30:18,520 --> 00:30:19,953
Two tons.

458
00:30:20,320 --> 00:30:21,958
Two tons?

459
00:30:22,120 --> 00:30:23,997
I thought I had a buyer,
and at the last minute,

460
00:30:24,160 --> 00:30:25,309
he backed out on me.

461
00:30:26,480 --> 00:30:27,549
Oh, well.

462
00:30:28,280 --> 00:30:29,793
- Say, Ben--
- No.

463
00:30:36,240 --> 00:30:38,629
- Hard at work, I see.
- Hey.

464
00:30:38,800 --> 00:30:41,109
- Oh, just reviewing the case.
- Have a pecan.

465
00:30:41,280 --> 00:30:42,759
So where are we?

466
00:30:42,920 --> 00:30:44,512
- In trouble.
- Well, the word I get

467
00:30:44,680 --> 00:30:46,989
is that Janice Pearson
and Sonny Hutton have been an item

468
00:30:47,160 --> 00:30:49,958
- for quite some time.
- Well, I'll tell you what,

469
00:30:50,120 --> 00:30:51,917
we can take all the stuff we've got

470
00:30:52,080 --> 00:30:55,390
and maybe put it in a gossip column,
but we'd never influence the jury.

471
00:30:55,760 --> 00:30:58,832
We've got three people
all with a possible motive.

472
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:00,752
Janice was at a party till 4:00.

473
00:31:00,920 --> 00:31:02,956
Danny Blaster
was at some club all night.

474
00:31:03,120 --> 00:31:05,350
Sonny Hutton wasn't even in town
the night of the murder.

475
00:31:05,520 --> 00:31:08,239
Well, we've got to break one
of their alibis, but they all check out.

476
00:31:08,880 --> 00:31:11,713
Well, I have a strong feeling

477
00:31:11,880 --> 00:31:15,077
this murder has something to do
with Sonny Hutton's tape.

478
00:31:15,240 --> 00:31:17,276
- You know, the one that's missing?
- Yeah.

479
00:31:18,080 --> 00:31:19,308
You got a clue?

480
00:31:19,840 --> 00:31:21,068
No.

481
00:31:21,760 --> 00:31:23,478
You're right. We're in trouble.

482
00:31:23,640 --> 00:31:25,756
Well, I'm going home.
I'll see you tomorrow.

483
00:31:25,920 --> 00:31:27,592
Okay.

484
00:31:27,760 --> 00:31:29,478
You know...

485
00:31:29,840 --> 00:31:32,479
...it's too bad somebody
can't get inside Sonny's hotel room,

486
00:31:33,000 --> 00:31:36,151
- take a look around.
- Yeah, it's too bad.

487
00:31:37,440 --> 00:31:39,112
Sure is.

488
00:31:48,960 --> 00:31:52,396
Excuse me,
could you turn my bed down?

489
00:32:01,680 --> 00:32:02,999
Thank you.

490
00:32:20,040 --> 00:32:21,189
Good night.

491
00:33:19,160 --> 00:33:20,798
Nice view, huh?

492
00:33:24,600 --> 00:33:28,957
As a rule do attorneys
break into other people's hotel rooms?

493
00:33:29,760 --> 00:33:31,830
What room is this?

494
00:33:32,000 --> 00:33:33,433
What room is this?

495
00:33:34,800 --> 00:33:38,190
- Fifteen-twenty-seven.
- Fifteen--? I check into 1 427.

496
00:33:38,360 --> 00:33:40,749
I guess I just made
an honest mistake.

497
00:33:41,680 --> 00:33:43,193
Are you looking for something?

498
00:33:43,360 --> 00:33:45,271
I'll be running along.

499
00:33:45,440 --> 00:33:46,998
No, no. No, I don't think so.

500
00:33:47,160 --> 00:33:48,593
Look, don't get cute.

501
00:33:49,320 --> 00:33:50,878
Room service.

502
00:33:51,520 --> 00:33:52,919
I didn't order room service.

503
00:33:53,280 --> 00:33:55,669
You didn't order a lobster
with a side order of grits?

504
00:33:56,320 --> 00:33:57,309
No.

505
00:33:57,840 --> 00:34:01,355
- Mercy, mercy, me, oh, my.
- Good night.

506
00:34:04,960 --> 00:34:06,552
You could've told me
you were coming here.

507
00:34:06,720 --> 00:34:08,631
So could you,
but I'm glad you did, partner.

508
00:34:09,160 --> 00:34:11,754
- Did you find the tape?
- No, but I did find this.

509
00:34:11,920 --> 00:34:13,592
He did it, Tyler.
I just know he's our guy,

510
00:34:13,760 --> 00:34:15,239
maybe this can prove it.

511
00:34:20,160 --> 00:34:22,754
- Hey, Angel.
- Sorry, not now. I'm running late.

512
00:34:22,920 --> 00:34:25,559
What really happened?
Come on, for your fans, Angel.

513
00:34:25,720 --> 00:34:27,392
- Angel?
- Yeah?

514
00:34:27,800 --> 00:34:30,678
For your fans, Angel, come on.
Did you do it?

515
00:34:31,000 --> 00:34:33,468
Angel, can we possibly
get an interview with you?

516
00:34:34,600 --> 00:34:36,477
You've got to be kidding.

517
00:34:46,800 --> 00:34:48,950
- Where's Tyler?
- I haven't heard from him.

518
00:34:49,120 --> 00:34:51,350
We'd better hear from him soon.

519
00:34:56,040 --> 00:34:57,155
Nice dress.

520
00:34:57,960 --> 00:35:00,155
Yeah, if you're Nancy Reagan.

521
00:35:00,680 --> 00:35:03,990
Division 21 Superior Court
of Fulton County is now in session.

522
00:35:04,160 --> 00:35:05,957
The Honourable
Richard Cooksey presiding.

523
00:35:06,120 --> 00:35:07,314
All rise.

524
00:35:13,640 --> 00:35:14,629
Mr. Matlock?

525
00:35:14,800 --> 00:35:17,314
I call Daniel Blaster to the stand.

526
00:35:18,760 --> 00:35:19,749
Now,

527
00:35:20,560 --> 00:35:22,471
Mr. Blaster,

528
00:35:22,640 --> 00:35:24,710
Mr. Pearson...

529
00:35:25,440 --> 00:35:27,158
...was your manager

530
00:35:27,320 --> 00:35:30,790
for eight years. Is that correct?

531
00:35:30,960 --> 00:35:32,598
Yes.

532
00:35:33,880 --> 00:35:34,949
Did you trust him?

533
00:35:35,560 --> 00:35:39,348
Lou was a manager and a friend.

534
00:35:39,520 --> 00:35:43,798
Mr. Blaster, isn't it true that you
hired an accounting firm to audit

535
00:35:43,960 --> 00:35:46,349
the books of this man
you trusted like a friend?

536
00:35:46,520 --> 00:35:48,511
It happens all the time
in the record business.

537
00:35:49,560 --> 00:35:52,996
Mr. Blaster, isn't it true that
Mr. Pearson threatened you

538
00:35:53,160 --> 00:35:54,593
if you proceeded with this audit?

539
00:35:55,240 --> 00:35:56,832
Lou was just kidding around.

540
00:35:57,600 --> 00:36:01,195
Is that why you hired bodyguards
two days before the audit began?

541
00:36:01,680 --> 00:36:05,275
I needed extra security for my tour.

542
00:36:06,360 --> 00:36:10,512
Mr. Blaster, isn't it true when you
played Los Angeles last year,

543
00:36:10,680 --> 00:36:11,669
you publicly accused

544
00:36:11,840 --> 00:36:13,796
Mr. Pearson of stealing from you

545
00:36:13,960 --> 00:36:16,235
and you were engaged in a
fistfight in your dressing room?

546
00:36:16,400 --> 00:36:17,753
Hey, I just got a little excited.

547
00:36:18,440 --> 00:36:22,069
This sort of thing happens all the time.
Lou knew it didn't mean anything.

548
00:36:22,240 --> 00:36:23,753
Where were you
the night of the murder?

549
00:36:24,520 --> 00:36:25,839
With friends.

550
00:36:27,080 --> 00:36:30,231
Would these friends
swear as to your whereabouts?

551
00:36:30,760 --> 00:36:31,988
Sure.

552
00:36:32,720 --> 00:36:35,359
Are these gentlemen
in the front row your friends?

553
00:36:35,520 --> 00:36:37,670
Can you gentlemen
stand up please?

554
00:36:40,440 --> 00:36:44,274
Would they go under the names of...

555
00:36:45,240 --> 00:36:46,912
...Snake Barker,

556
00:36:47,080 --> 00:36:52,279
Trashcan Reed,
Bones Lloyd, and Doctor Death?

557
00:36:55,440 --> 00:36:56,839
Right.

558
00:36:58,080 --> 00:36:59,513
No further questions.

559
00:37:04,960 --> 00:37:06,916
- Anything from Tyler?
- Not yet.

560
00:37:07,080 --> 00:37:08,638
Your witness, Mr. Park.

561
00:37:09,080 --> 00:37:11,196
Mr. Blaster, are you familiar
with the property

562
00:37:11,360 --> 00:37:14,909
at 321 2 Crestline Drive
in Malibu, California?

563
00:37:15,080 --> 00:37:16,195
It's my beach house.

564
00:37:16,360 --> 00:37:18,112
Are you in the habit
of loaning out that house

565
00:37:18,280 --> 00:37:20,874
- to your friends and associates?
- Sure.

566
00:37:21,040 --> 00:37:22,075
In April of last year,

567
00:37:22,240 --> 00:37:23,798
did you loan out
the use of that house

568
00:37:23,960 --> 00:37:25,473
to Miss Danello?

569
00:37:32,200 --> 00:37:34,555
- Mr. Blaster!
- Yes.

570
00:37:36,720 --> 00:37:38,517
She stayed there.

571
00:37:38,680 --> 00:37:39,954
Was she alone?

572
00:37:40,120 --> 00:37:41,439
No.

573
00:37:44,080 --> 00:37:46,036
And who was
staying there with her?

574
00:37:46,800 --> 00:37:48,119
Lou Pearson.

575
00:37:53,880 --> 00:37:55,791
No further questions.

576
00:37:56,360 --> 00:37:57,349
Your Honour,

577
00:37:57,520 --> 00:37:59,829
with the court's permission,
I'd like to take a few minutes

578
00:38:00,000 --> 00:38:01,274
to confer with my client.

579
00:38:01,440 --> 00:38:04,000
The court will take
a ten-minute recess.

580
00:38:08,240 --> 00:38:10,117
Why didn't you tell me
you were having an affair

581
00:38:10,280 --> 00:38:12,316
with Lou Pearson when
I asked you the first time?

582
00:38:12,480 --> 00:38:14,516
What happened between Lou and me
was a long time ago

583
00:38:14,680 --> 00:38:15,908
and it's none of your business.

584
00:38:16,080 --> 00:38:17,433
I got caught with my pants down.

585
00:38:17,600 --> 00:38:19,192
I made a mistake, okay?

586
00:38:19,360 --> 00:38:24,593
When am I going to get it through
your head I am trying to save your life?

587
00:38:29,560 --> 00:38:31,676
Why do you really care, anyway?

588
00:38:41,840 --> 00:38:43,432
I'm sorry.

589
00:38:43,600 --> 00:38:44,749
It's all right.

590
00:38:46,400 --> 00:38:48,072
I know you're scared.

591
00:38:51,400 --> 00:38:52,435
Tyler's here.

592
00:39:05,680 --> 00:39:06,749
How's business?

593
00:39:08,040 --> 00:39:09,837
Business is great.

594
00:39:10,000 --> 00:39:14,232
I understand your record sales
have fallen off lately.

595
00:39:15,080 --> 00:39:16,274
A little.

596
00:39:17,320 --> 00:39:20,232
The fact is you haven't
had a new album in

597
00:39:20,400 --> 00:39:21,913
two years, isn't that correct?

598
00:39:22,080 --> 00:39:23,593
That's not unusual.

599
00:39:23,760 --> 00:39:25,432
But it is true, isn't it?

600
00:39:26,480 --> 00:39:27,913
Yes, it's true.

601
00:39:28,800 --> 00:39:31,712
I bet Lou Pearson
wasn't very happy about that.

602
00:39:32,160 --> 00:39:34,230
Lou wasn't worried.
We just finished another album.

603
00:39:35,120 --> 00:39:38,237
Yeah, that's right.
That was the tape you sent him.

604
00:39:38,640 --> 00:39:40,392
- Yes.
- The one that was stolen

605
00:39:40,560 --> 00:39:41,754
the night of the murder.

606
00:39:42,400 --> 00:39:43,389
That's right.

607
00:39:43,560 --> 00:39:45,039
Would you like to get it back?

608
00:39:45,400 --> 00:39:48,233
Of course I would. It's very valuable.
It's the only mix that exists.

609
00:39:49,600 --> 00:39:51,079
The only one?

610
00:39:51,240 --> 00:39:52,798
That's taking quite a chance, isn't it?

611
00:39:54,160 --> 00:39:57,709
There's a 24-track master
at Lou's studio.

612
00:39:57,880 --> 00:40:00,394
The one that was stolen
is the only mix.

613
00:40:00,560 --> 00:40:01,879
Oh, I see, I see, I see.

614
00:40:04,360 --> 00:40:08,592
Did you and Lou Pearson have a fight
over what he thought of your album?

615
00:40:10,400 --> 00:40:12,197
I never saw Lou.

616
00:40:12,360 --> 00:40:15,113
I got into Atlanta the morning
after the murder.

617
00:40:15,280 --> 00:40:16,269
That's right.

618
00:40:16,440 --> 00:40:17,429
You said you flew in

619
00:40:17,600 --> 00:40:19,716
- directly from New York, didn't you?
- Yes.

620
00:40:20,320 --> 00:40:23,073
On Georgia Air flight...

621
00:40:23,240 --> 00:40:25,117
...1 83.

622
00:40:25,600 --> 00:40:26,999
That's right.

623
00:40:31,560 --> 00:40:34,836
You sure you weren't in Atlanta
the night Lou Pearson was murdered?

624
00:40:35,000 --> 00:40:35,989
Objection.

625
00:40:36,160 --> 00:40:37,912
The witness has already answered
the question.

626
00:40:38,080 --> 00:40:39,433
Sustained.

627
00:40:40,120 --> 00:40:41,838
Your Honour,

628
00:40:42,000 --> 00:40:44,116
at this time, I'd like
to introduce into evidence

629
00:40:44,280 --> 00:40:45,429
this claim check

630
00:40:45,600 --> 00:40:49,593
from a parking facility at the
Charleston, South Carolina Airport.

631
00:40:49,760 --> 00:40:50,749
Recognize it?

632
00:40:52,040 --> 00:40:53,029
No.

633
00:40:54,680 --> 00:40:56,830
Doesn't the flight you're on,
flight 1 83,

634
00:40:57,000 --> 00:40:59,309
make a stopover at Charleston?

635
00:41:00,520 --> 00:41:01,794
Yeah. So what?

636
00:41:02,200 --> 00:41:03,872
Mr. Hutton,

637
00:41:04,040 --> 00:41:05,951
it just so happens

638
00:41:06,120 --> 00:41:10,432
that a car you rented in New York
is at this very moment

639
00:41:10,600 --> 00:41:12,795
parked at the Charleston Airport.

640
00:41:19,720 --> 00:41:22,280
Oh, God. You know what I think?

641
00:41:22,440 --> 00:41:24,192
I think you drove that car

642
00:41:24,360 --> 00:41:28,035
from New York to Atlanta,
then back up to Charleston,

643
00:41:28,200 --> 00:41:33,035
parked it, got on flight 1 83 to make
it appear you flew in from New York.

644
00:41:33,200 --> 00:41:34,394
Objection.

645
00:41:34,560 --> 00:41:36,630
- Pure speculation.
- Sustained.

646
00:41:38,600 --> 00:41:40,318
Mr. Hutton,

647
00:41:40,480 --> 00:41:42,710
didn't you see Lou Pearson
about your album?

648
00:41:43,760 --> 00:41:46,035
I told you I didn't see Lou.

649
00:41:46,200 --> 00:41:49,795
Isn't it true that he knew you were
having an affair with his wife?

650
00:41:50,200 --> 00:41:51,189
No.

651
00:41:51,360 --> 00:41:53,954
Isn't it true he refused
to release your album?

652
00:41:54,440 --> 00:41:56,158
- No.
- Isn't it true he promised

653
00:41:56,320 --> 00:41:58,675
to destroy your career,
you fought with him, and killed him?

654
00:41:58,840 --> 00:41:59,909
That is a lie.

655
00:42:00,080 --> 00:42:01,069
Mr. Hutton...

656
00:42:03,560 --> 00:42:06,472
...if that missing tape
were found in your rental car,

657
00:42:06,640 --> 00:42:09,552
wouldn't that prove
you were in Atlanta

658
00:42:09,720 --> 00:42:12,439
and did see Lou Pearson
the night of the murder?

659
00:42:17,080 --> 00:42:18,513
Wouldn't it, Mr. Hutton?

660
00:42:25,120 --> 00:42:28,032
Would you like my associate
to play the tape for you?

661
00:42:34,240 --> 00:42:35,468
Mr. Hutton...

662
00:42:37,120 --> 00:42:39,429
...not 20 minutes ago,

663
00:42:39,600 --> 00:42:42,797
my associate returned
from Charleston,

664
00:42:42,960 --> 00:42:45,633
where he found your rental car,

665
00:42:46,600 --> 00:42:51,230
had keys made, opened the trunk,

666
00:42:51,400 --> 00:42:54,676
looked inside, there it was.

667
00:42:56,880 --> 00:42:57,949
I repeat.

668
00:42:59,440 --> 00:43:01,670
Would you like us to play it for you?

669
00:43:09,120 --> 00:43:10,348
No.

670
00:43:18,520 --> 00:43:21,239
In other words, Mr. Hutton,

671
00:43:21,400 --> 00:43:24,233
you lied about
when you arrived in Atlanta.

672
00:43:24,400 --> 00:43:27,915
You lied about seeing Lou Pearson.

673
00:43:28,080 --> 00:43:29,718
You lied about the tape.

674
00:43:31,120 --> 00:43:32,838
You are a liar.

675
00:43:34,480 --> 00:43:36,516
And maybe something a lot worse.

676
00:43:45,480 --> 00:43:47,357
No further questions, Your Honour.

677
00:44:04,520 --> 00:44:06,431
We find the defendant not guilty.

678
00:44:09,400 --> 00:44:10,833
Order! Order!

679
00:44:20,600 --> 00:44:21,999
You play poker, Matlock?

680
00:44:22,160 --> 00:44:23,434
On occasion.

681
00:44:24,080 --> 00:44:25,399
Blue button.

682
00:44:26,320 --> 00:44:30,950
<i>Master dub 1 29 7 7, artist</i>
<i>Sonny Hutton, recorded June 1 st.</i>

683
00:44:32,560 --> 00:44:34,994
Sounds like a full house to me.

684
00:44:42,720 --> 00:44:44,233
Thanks.

685
00:44:44,400 --> 00:44:45,799
I owe you two.

686
00:44:45,960 --> 00:44:48,793
- Anytime.
- Anywhere.

687
00:44:52,480 --> 00:44:57,429
You know, for a lawyer, you're okay.

688
00:44:59,320 --> 00:45:01,470
I won't argue with that.

689
00:45:03,520 --> 00:45:04,714
Thank you.

690
00:45:12,040 --> 00:45:13,837
Well, Tyler,

691
00:45:14,000 --> 00:45:16,355
that was a very successful
trip to Charleston.

692
00:45:16,800 --> 00:45:17,994
It sure was.

693
00:45:18,160 --> 00:45:19,832
You know,
I met this guy at the airport

694
00:45:20,000 --> 00:45:23,072
who's got a truckload
of frozen pie crusts.

695
00:45:23,240 --> 00:45:24,559
I get my hands on those suckers,

696
00:45:24,720 --> 00:45:27,917
I'm going into the
Christmas pecan pie business.

697
00:45:28,080 --> 00:45:29,832
You know what?

698
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:32,309
I was driving through
this little town one time.

699
00:45:32,480 --> 00:45:33,629
It wasn't really a town.

700
00:45:33,800 --> 00:45:36,758
It was just a general store
with a gas pump out front.

701
00:45:36,920 --> 00:45:38,672
And while I was
gassing up, I went in

702
00:45:38,840 --> 00:45:40,831
and I saw the walls
of the general store

703
00:45:41,000 --> 00:45:44,072
were just lined
with these bags of salt.

704
00:45:44,240 --> 00:45:47,312
So I said to the fellow, I said,
''You must sell a lot of salt.''

705
00:45:47,480 --> 00:45:50,392
He said, ''No, I don't sell much salt.

706
00:45:50,560 --> 00:45:52,869
But the fellow that sells me salt,
now, he sells salt.''

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
